          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Chiang Kai-shek</strong><br>\nChiang Kai-shek was born on October 31, 1887, in Xikou, a town in southeastern China. As a young man, he pursued military studies in Japan, where he was influenced by the principles of discipline and order. He returned to China in 1911 to join the revolutionary forces aiming to overthrow the Qing Dynasty, which was the last imperial dynasty of China.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Military Rise</strong><br>\nChiang Kai-shek quickly rose through the ranks due to his military skill and dedication. After the death of Sun Yat-sen, the leader of the Kuomintang (KMT), or Nationalist Party, Chiang succeeded him as the commander of the National Revolutionary Army and became a prominent political leader.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Northern Expedition</strong><br>\nIn 1926, Chiang Kai-shek launched the Northern Expedition, a military campaign designed to unify China under KMT rule. The expedition was successful, leading to the nominal unification of China and the establishment of a national government with Chiang at its helm.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
