          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Nanjing Decade</strong><br>\nChiang Kai-shek's rule from 1927 to 1937, known as the Nanjing Decade, was marked by efforts to modernize China and suppress communists and warlords. Despite his attempts to stabilize the country, his government faced criticism for corruption and ineffectiveness in improving the lives of the rural poor.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Conflict with the Communists</strong><br>\nThroughout the 1930s, Chiang's government was embroiled in a civil war with the Chinese Communist Party. Despite initial successes, Chiang's focus on the communist threat over the invasion by Japan in the Second Sino-Japanese War was seen as a strategic misstep.</p>\n\n<p><strong>World War II and the Alliance with the Allies</strong><br>\nDuring World War II, Chiang Kai-shek aligned China with the Allies against Japan. His leadership was vital in resisting Japanese aggression, but his government was weakened by internal strife and corruption.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
