          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Civil War and Loss to Mao Zedong</strong><br>\nAfter World War II, civil war resumed between the KMT and the communists. Despite initial advantages and support from the United States, Chiang's forces were ultimately defeated by Mao Zedong's communists, leading to the KMT's retreat to Taiwan in 1949.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Leadership in Taiwan</strong><br>\nIn Taiwan, Chiang Kai-shek established a government in exile, which he led until his death. He focused on economic development and maintaining a strict political regime, which laid the groundwork for Taiwan's future growth.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nChiang Kai-shek died on April 5, 1975. His legacy is complex; he is remembered for his role in unifying and leading China through turbulent times, yet also for his authoritarian rule and the political repression that occurred under his regime.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
